Paget's Disease of Bone
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Deep resting bone pain, Enlarging skull, Bowed tibiae

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-041
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of deep, unrelenting bone pain in the pelvis and thigh, causing complete sleep deprivation and sudden inability to weight-bear.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly patient with a known history of Paget's disease experiencing a sudden, severe escalation in localized deep bone pain, rendering them unable to walk or sleep. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for an acute pathological microfracture, rule out secondary osteosarcoma (rare but critical), and administer potent analgesia to break the severe pain cycle prior to daytime metabolic and orthopedic review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hip and thigh bone are aching so deeply it feels like a toothache inside my leg; it kept me awake all night and now I can't put any weight on it."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of polyostotic Paget's disease presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right pelvic and femoral pain. Timeline: Baseline constant, dull, aching bone pain localized to the right pelvis and femur for the past year; drastically worsened over the last 48 hours. The pain is now described as deep, boring, and unrelenting, noticeably worse at night, and explicitly lacks relief with rest. Patient reports a sudden, sharp "catch" in the mid-thigh when attempting to stand this morning, resulting in a complete refusal to weight-bear. Patient notes a chronic history of progressive anterolateral bowing of the right femur and an increasing hat size over the last decade.
Functional Status: Acutely chair-bound; utilizing a walking frame but unable to load the right leg due to severe pain inhibition.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; severe sleep deprivation secondary to unremitting nocturnal bone pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly exhausted, sitting asymmetrically to offload the right pelvis.
Physical Exam: Right lower limb reveals pronounced anterolateral bowing of the femur. There is localized, abnormal warmth palpated directly over the mid-thigh (hypervascular pagetic bone segment) compared to the contralateral side. Exquisite, deep bony point tenderness is elicited upon direct compression of the lateral femoral shaft. Pain is severely exacerbated by axial loading (gentle heel strike). Hip joint range of motion (internal/external rotation) is preserved but guarded, indicating the pain is primarily osseous rather than articular. Cranial exam reveals notable frontal bossing. Neurological exam of the lower limbs is intact.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting in a supportive armchair; unable to safely transfer to the bedroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Baseline Paracetamol and oral NSAIDs utilized with zero effect on the deep, boring bone pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute skeletal pain crisis in Paget's Disease of Bone - Status: Exacerbated (High index of suspicion for pathological microfracture)
Differentials:

Pathological Fracture (Femoral Shaft) - Highly suspected given the sudden sharp "catch" and inability to weight-bear, requiring urgent radiographic confirmation.

Hip Joint Osteoarthritis - Ruled out clinically as the primary cause of the acute crisis, given the maximal tenderness is mid-shaft on the femur and passive joint rotation is preserved.

Osteosarcoma (Pagetic Degeneration) - A rare but critical differential for a sudden, severe pain spike in established Paget's, requiring formal imaging and specialist follow-up.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, acute exacerbation of deep, resting bone pain characteristic of active Paget's disease, complicated by a new, sharp, mechanically loaded pain suggesting a potential pathological microfracture through the weakened, disorganized bone. The severe nocturnal pain had caused profound exhaustion, and the inability to weight-bear rendered the patient functionally unsafe. Delaying evaluation would have subjected the patient to unmanaged, agonizing bone pain and the high risk of converting a microfracture into a complete, displaced fracture during an unassisted transfer. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the osseous nature of the pain, bypassed inappropriate musculoskeletal treatments, and permitted the immediate initiation of high-dose analgesia and rigid mechanical offloading.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT. Prescribed oral Tapentadol 50mg BD for ongoing severe acute bone pain.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use the provided bilateral axillary crutches to maintain a strictly non-weight-bearing status on the right leg for all transfers. Consequence: Complete mechanical offloading prevents the weakened, bowed bone from physically bending under body weight, immediately stopping the sharp, agonizing "catching" pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Sleep in the recliner chair tonight rather than attempting the painful transfer to bed. Consequence: Avoidance of the excruciating, twisting forces on the pelvis and femur that occur when trying to lie flat and turn over, allowing the strong painkillers to secure a block of sleep (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted and supplied bilateral axillary crutches.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review first thing in the morning for STAT plain radiography of the femur/pelvis and urgent metabolic bone specialist referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation via ambulance if there is a sudden, explosive snap in the thigh, if the leg becomes visibly shortened or externally rotated, or if severe, uncontrollable pain breaks through the medication.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of a microfracture is clinically suspected; an X-ray is absolutely mandatory to assess the structural integrity of the bone. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute pain crisis will be blunted by the strong medications, but the underlying bone weakness remains. Long-term management requires specialized infusions (bisphosphonates) to slow down the hyperactive bone turnover.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you feel a loud crack in your leg, if the shape of your thigh suddenly changes, or if you cannot manage the pain even while resting completely still.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your Paget's disease makes your bones grow rapidly but in a very disorganized, weak way. The sudden sharp pain today suggests the bone may have developed a tiny crack (microfracture) under your body weight. The strong painkiller will dull the deep ache, but we must protect the bone from fully breaking.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely no weight on the right leg. Use the crutches to hop. Do not attempt to walk on it, even if the painkillers make it feel better. You must get an X-ray tomorrow morning.

